<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Constantius II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">317–<a href="/years/361">361 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Constantius II was a Roman emperor of the fourth century CE, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine I</a>. He fought prolonged wars on the eastern frontier against the Sasanian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-ii">Shapur II</a>. Shapur later recovered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nisibis">Nisibis</a> and other territories from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in the treaty of 363 CE that followed Julian's failed invasion.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/constantius-ii.jpg" alt="Constantius II" width="296" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mary Harrsch · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_Constantius_II_(Mary_Harrsch).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Constantius was born on 7 August 317 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirmium">Sirmium</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a>, the third son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine the Great</a> and the second by his wife Fausta. His father made him caesar on 8 November 324, when he was seven. In 336 war broke out with Sasanian Persia under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-ii">Shapur II</a>, and Constantine sent him east to command the frontier. He defeated and killed the Persian general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narses">Narses</a> at Narasara, recaptured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amida">Amida</a> and strengthened its walls.</p><p>When Constantine died in 337, Constantius buried him in the Church of the Holy Apostles. Soon afterwards the army massacred two of his uncles and seven cousins, among them Hannibalianus and Dalmatius. The official account blamed a mutinous army, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus">Ammianus Marcellinus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zosimus">Zosimus</a>, Libanius, Athanasius and Julian blamed Constantius himself. He and his brothers Constantine II and Constans then divided the empire at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirmium">Sirmium</a>, and he received the eastern provinces.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>For years Constantius defended the eastern frontier against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-ii">Shapur II</a>, whose attacks were mostly sieges of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nisibis">Nisibis</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singara">Singara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amida">Amida</a>; Shapur won most of these encounters but gained little. In the west, Constantine II died in an ambush in 340, leaving Constans in control until he was killed in 350 by forces loyal to the usurper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnentius">Magnentius</a>. Constantius made his cousin Gallus caesar in the east and married his sister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirta">Constantina</a> to him. He defeated Magnentius at Mursa Major in 351, one of the bloodiest battles between Roman armies, and at Mons Seleucus in 353, after which Magnentius killed himself.</p><p>Sole ruler from 353, he had Gallus arrested and executed after reports of his conduct, and on 6 November 355 made his last male relative, Julian, caesar and sent him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>. He campaigned against the Alamanni, and in 357 against the Sarmatians and Quadi across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>; that year he visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> for the only time. Laws of the 350s forbade pagan sacrifice under penalty of death, though he never disbanded the priestly colleges. He pressed a compromise position between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arianism">Arianism</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicene-creed">Nicene Creed</a> on the church, and he restricted Jewish ownership of slaves.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>When Shapur destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singara">Singara</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amida">Amida</a> in 360, Constantius travelled east. Julian's Gallic legions, ordered to send reinforcements, proclaimed him augustus. Constantius could not answer at once and in 361 set out west, but he fell ill with fever and died at Mopsuestia on 3 November 361. Sources say he was baptised by the bishop Euzoius and named Julian his successor. He was buried in the Church of the Holy Apostles.</p><p>His reputation is divided. Ammianus portrays a conscientious but vain emperor open to flatterers, while orthodox churchmen and Julian saw a heretic and a murderer. Some modern historians think he suffered at the hands of unsympathetic authors, and note that many contemporaries held him in high esteem.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Constantius II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 350s, during the civil war between <b>Constantius II</b> and Magnentius, Alemannic kings overran the Rhine frontier and occupied land on its west bank.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alemanni">Alemanni · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Constantius II</b> campaigned against the Sarmatians and Limigantes in 358–359 CE, installed a client king and took the title Sarmaticus.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sarmatians">Sarmatians · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After decades of war against <b>Constantius II</b> and Julian he obtained, in the Peace of 363 concluded with Jovian after Julian's death on campaign, the cession of Nisibis, Singara, five provinces beyond the Tigris and fifteen fortresses, the most favourable settlement Persia ever won from Rome.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…known as the Magna Ecclesia or Great Church for its size, was consecrated in 360, in the reign of the emperor <b>Constantius II</b>, by the Arian bishop Eudoxius of Antioch; it had a timber roof and stood next to the area where the Great Palace was being developed.</blockquote><a href="/work/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In 323 CE the Roman emperor Constantine the Great recognised the Christian religion, and in 356 <b>Constantius II</b> ordered the closing of pagan temples throughout the Roman Empire, into which Egypt had been annexed in 30 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/work/karnak">Karnak · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>In 356/57 the emperor <b>Constantius II</b> asked for Frumentius to be sent to Alexandria for examination and replaced, but the request came to nothing.</blockquote><a href="/societies/aksum">Social Hierarchy of the Kingdom of Aksum</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirmium">Sirmium</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.97,19.61&amp;year=345" aria-label="Sirmium on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5017 576 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Constantius II"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5467 846h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5467 846h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.97,19.61&amp;year=345">What was here in 345 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-family"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Family</h2><ul class="enc-seealso enc-fam"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/constantine-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Constantine I</span><span class="tp">Father &#183; c. 272–337 CE</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="ft-note">Parents, spouses and children as Wikidata records them, among the people of the encyclopedia: not the whole family.</p></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I entreat you to listen attentively to what I will state with all the brevity possible. For the language of truth is always concise and simple.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech to his army (Ammianus Marcellinus, History, book 14) &#183; tr. C. D. Yonge, 1862 &#183; Ammianus' account &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/28587/pg28587-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
